# AUR-4037: release retention, decoupled from the deploy path.
#
# prune-releases.sh already implements the policy correctly and is well
# guarded (release-guard.test.sh + prune-releases.test.sh, 46 cases). The
# defect this unit fixes is not the policy, it is the TRIGGER: retention only
# ever ran as the tail step of a successful build-release.sh, and that script
# skips pruning — silently, exit 0 — on three separate conditions (unresolvable
# `current`, lock held by a concurrent deploy, unopenable lock). Nothing
# retried, nothing alarmed, and no other deploy path called it at all. Since
# AUR-4155 refuses `build-release.sh --activate`, activation moved to
# safe-deploy.sh / hand-rolled finish-deploy scripts, so in practice the one
# trigger retention had was frequently not on the path taken.
#
# Measured consequence (2026-08-05): 5 releases x 2.0 GB = 10 GB on a disk at
# 92%, where the policy's own ceiling is 3 (running/current + previous + 1
# rollback). 4.0 GB was stranded by a policy that was correct and simply never
# fired.
#
# Retention that depends on a build succeeding AND winning a lock is not
# retention. This runs it on a clock instead, so a skip costs one interval
# rather than being permanent.
#
# Installed by scripts/deploy/install-release-prune-timer.sh. Runs
# system-level (not as a paperclip.service child) so it survives control-plane
# restarts, and points at the ACTIVE RELEASE — same convention as the drift and
# auto-deploy units — so it tracks whatever retention policy is deployed.
[Unit]
Description=Paperclip release retention (prune old /opt/paperclip/app/releases)
Documentation=https://paperclip/AUR/issues/AUR-4037
Wants=network-online.target
After=network-online.target

[Service]
Type=oneshot
User=ievgen
Group=ievgen
ExecStart=/opt/paperclip/app/current/scripts/deploy/prune-releases.sh
StandardOutput=journal
StandardError=journal
SyslogIdentifier=paperclip-release-prune
